One of the most commonly asked Kubernetes questions in interviews is:

𝗤. 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗿𝗲𝗰𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗶𝘀𝘀𝘂𝗲 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗳𝗮𝗰𝗲𝗱 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗵𝗼𝘄 𝗱𝗶𝗱 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗳𝗶𝘅 𝗶𝘁?

𝐈𝐬𝐬𝐮𝐞:
A production microservice running on EKS suddenly started failing to access S3 and Secrets Manager.
The application logs showed:
AccessDenied: Not authorized to perform sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity

🔍 𝐑𝐨𝐨𝐭 𝐂𝐚𝐮𝐬𝐞 𝐀𝐧𝐚𝐥𝐲𝐬𝐢𝐬
After investigation, I found,
The pod was using the default service account instead of the intended one
The IAM Role trust policy had a mismatch in:
namespace
service account name

The OIDC provider URL was incorrectly configured in the IAM role trust policy

🛠️ 𝐅𝐢𝐱 𝐀𝐩𝐩𝐥𝐢𝐞𝐝 (𝐒𝐭𝐞𝐩-𝐛𝐲-𝐒𝐭𝐞𝐩)

1️⃣ Verified Service Account
𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝙜𝘦𝘵 𝘱𝘰𝘥 <𝘱𝘰𝘥-𝘯𝘢𝘮𝘦> -𝘰 𝘺𝘢𝘮𝘭 | 𝙜𝘳𝘦𝘱 𝘴𝘦𝘳𝘷𝘪𝘤𝘦𝘈𝘤𝘤𝘰𝘶𝘯𝘵𝘕𝘢𝘮𝘦

➡️ It was using default instead of app-sa

2️⃣ 𝐅𝐢𝐱𝐞𝐝 𝐃𝐞𝐩𝐥𝐨𝐲𝐦𝐞𝐧𝐭
serviceAccountName: app-sa

3️⃣ 𝐅𝐢𝐱𝐞𝐝 𝐈𝐀𝐌 𝐓𝐫𝐮𝐬𝐭 𝐏𝐨𝐥𝐢𝐜𝐲
Corrected this in the IAM role:

"Condition": {
  "StringEquals": {
    "oidc.eks.<region>.amazonaws.com/id/<OIDC_ID>:sub":
    "system:serviceaccount:prod:app-sa"
  }
}

4️⃣ 𝐑𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐫𝐭𝐞𝐝 𝐏𝐨𝐝𝐬
kubectl rollout restart deployment app-deployment

Boom 💥
Service recovered. No secrets. No access keys.
